+### Keep observer completion separate from resource completion
+
+Java 11 `CompletableFuture.orTimeout` and `completeOnTimeout` mutate their receiver. Applying one
+to the future used to release a permit can make the release callback run while protected work
+continues. Ordinary `CompletableFuture.cancel(true)` also does not prove the computation stopped.
+Keep the cleanup lifecycle private; give callers a separate observer when they can complete,
+cancel or time out their result independently.
+
+Partial Java 11 sketch, after successful acquisition and launch: `actualDone` is a private, plain
+`CompletableFuture<Result>` completed only after the protected operation's real cleanup, on success
+or failure. `lease` is the acquired lease and `remainingNanos` comes from the existing deadline.
+Neither `actualDone` nor the stage carrying release is exposed to caller mutation:
+
+```java
+CompletableFuture<Result> released =
+    actualDone.whenComplete((value, failure) -> lease.close());
+return released.copy().orTimeout(remainingNanos, TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
+```
+
+The copy protects the private lifecycle from observer mutation; it does not cancel the work.
+If cancellation is required, invoke the client's cancellation protocol and retain the permit until
+its documented termination/cleanup signal. Verify custom/client future semantics; copying an
+arbitrary client future does not turn it into a cleanup signal. If no trustworthy terminal signal
+exists, this sketch cannot establish a hard resource bound. Preserve that gap rather than releasing
+on a timer. For streaming responses, apply the longer ownership interval below.
